From d253001575891cbce726011772767a2e564a4f14 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 04:50:34 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 52/80] kosmickrisp: clamp the cube an array coordinate selects

A VK_EXT_non_seamless_cube_map sampler is served by sampling the same
storage through a 2D array alias of the constituent cube faces, so the
lowering turns a cube array coordinate into an array layer itself.  It
rounded the coordinate and scaled it by the six faces without clamping
it, leaving the product for Metal to clamp against the alias.  Vulkan
clamps the cube against the cube count before the faces are indexed, and
clamping afterwards is not the same thing: a coordinate one cube past the
end becomes an index six faces past the end, which lands on the last face
of the last cube instead of on the selected face of it.  A coordinate
below zero collapses onto the first face of the first cube the same way.

Round, clamp against the layer count the alias reports, and only then
scale by the six faces.

dEQP-VK.texture.shadow.cube_array.* sweeps the coordinate from just below
the first cube to well past the last one, so every such lookup outside the
range sampled the wrong face: 301805 of 306029 differing texels in a
native side by side comparison against the seamless path, which Metal
resolves natively.  The rest are mip level choices near a face boundary,
where the lowered path evaluates the projected derivative per fragment
instead of across the quad; both lie inside the level range the reference
allows.
